Subjective daytime fatigue and mild lethargy directly attributed to poor sleep hygiene
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Persistent daytime weariness, Documented inadequate sleep duration, Frequent yawning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-039
Reason for Visit: Severe, persistent fatigue requiring medical evaluation to rule out occult anemia, hypothyroidism, or major depressive disorder.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted profound daytime lethargy impairing daily function. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate for signs of severe anemia, perform a targeted neurological exam, and rule out potentially dangerous causes of acute fatigue (such as occult bleeding or severe metabolic derangement) before attributing symptoms to lifestyle factors.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am constantly exhausted, I can't concentrate, and I feel like I'm walking through molasses all day.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a persistent feeling of weariness throughout the waking hours. Reports difficulty initiating or maintaining nocturnal sleep, largely due to late-night screen use and irregular bedtimes. Notes frequent subjective yawning and a strong desire to rest during the day. Admits to documented inadequate total sleep duration (averaging 4-5 hours nightly). Complete absence of unintentional weight loss, night sweats, or focal pain. Maintains regular cognitive function without delirium.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but tasks require immense effort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Decreased concentration while driving or operating machinery.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 | HR: 72 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, yawning frequently, appears tired but in no acute distress.
Physical Exam: Physical examination reveals normal baseline cardiopulmonary effort and exercise tolerance. Diagnostic triad confirmed: persistent daytime weariness, documented inadequate sleep duration, and frequent yawning. Normal focal neurological examination without muscle weakness or localized deficits. Conjunctivae are pink, ruling out severe clinical anemia. Thyroid gland is not palpably enlarged. No lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Multiple electronic devices present on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient consumes high amounts of caffeine late in the afternoon.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subjective daytime fatigue and mild lethargy directly attributed to poor sleep hygiene - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Occult Anemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pink conjunctivae, lack of shortness of breath on exertion, and normal resting heart rate.

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of weight gain, cold intolerance, dry skin, or delayed deep tendon reflexes.

Obstructive Sleep Apnea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of witnessed apneas, lack of heavy snoring, and normal BMI, though a sleep study would definitively rule this out if symptoms persist.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to potential driving risks)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of profound fatigue required an urgent face-to-face assessment to safely exclude critical organic pathologies, such as symptomatic anemia, early heart failure, or severe metabolic disturbances. Delaying assessment carried the risk of missing signs of acute deterioration. A thorough clinical examination confirmed stable hemodynamics, intact neurology, and no obvious signs of organic disease, allowing the clinician to confidently link the lethargy to severe sleep deprivation and initiate lifestyle modifications.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the absence of immediate life-threatening pathology. Planned non-urgent laboratory panel (FBC, TSH, Iron studies) to be ordered by regular GP to definitively exclude organic causes if symptoms persist.
Positive Behavioral Change: Leave the smartphone and all digital devices charging in a separate room overnight, establishing the bedroom strictly as a screen-free zone to eliminate blue light exposure and prevent late-night stimulation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to review sleep diary and consider blood tests.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience sudden chest pain, severe shortness of breath, unexplained fainting, or notice black, tarry stools.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical based on lifestyle history; occult organic causes will be formally ruled out with routine blood tests if symptoms do not improve.
Clinical Course: Fatigue should improve significantly within 7 to 10 days of establishing a strict 8-hour sleep schedule.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you become so fatigued you cannot stand up, if you develop a fever, or if you begin losing weight rapidly without trying.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is severely sleep-deprived. Screens emit blue light that tricks your brain into thinking it is daytime, stopping the production of the sleep hormone melatonin. Caffeine consumed after 2 PM also stays in your system and blocks sleep signals.
Actionable Steps: Stop caffeine intake after midday. Remove all screens from the bedroom. Go to bed and wake up at the exact same time every day.

